@@s001 Vector spaces, independence and bases
@@s002 dato:standard basis e1 e2,due:Anders' basis a1 a2,ora:one piece of the combination,ok:the point P
@@s003 Peter is at the point <b>P = (4, 3)</b>. These coordinates refer to the <b>standard basis</b>: e1 = (1, 0) and e2 = (0, 1).
@@s004 (4, 3) means: <b>4 times e1 plus 3 times e2</b>. The coordinates are the weights of a linear combination of the vectors of the basis.
@@s005 Anders uses another basis: <b>a1 = (2, 1)</b> and <b>a2 = (1, 2)</b>. They are independent (they do not lie on the same line), so they are a good basis of the plane.
@@s006 In Anders' basis P has other weights: one looks for c₁, c₂ with c₁a1 + c₂a2 = P, that is the system <b>A x<sub>A</sub> = P</b> (A has a1 and a2 as columns). Solution: <b>x<sub>A</sub> = (5/3, 2/3)</b>.
@@s007 Check: 5/3 · a1 = (3.33, 1.67)…
@@s008 … plus 2/3 · a2 = (0.67, 1.33) gives exactly (4, 3). <b>Same point, different coordinates</b>: (4, 3) in the standard basis, (1.67, 0.67) in Anders'.
@@s009 dato:v₁,due:v₂,ora:span,no:adds nothing
@@s010 dato:v,due:u,ora:combination that gives 0
@@s011 dato:standard basis,due:Bertha's basis,ora:the two pieces,ok:the point P
@@s012 dato:independent columns,no:dependent column,ok:unique solution
@@s013 Vector spaces, independence and bases
@@s014 Week 6 (Grasple exercises) and lecture 6 ("change of basis"). A vector can be described with different coordinates according to the <b>basis</b> chosen. It is the key of PCA (week 12) and explains when least squares has a single solution. DW chapters 9 and 10; exam question <b>Q4 (d)</b>.
@@s015 Span: everything that can be reached
@@s016 From linear combinations to the span
@@s017 A <b>linear combination</b>: each vector multiplied by a number, then the sum.
@@s018 span(v₁, …, vₙ) = { <mark class="ok">all</mark> the linear combinations }
@@s019 All the vectors that can be built by varying the weights. It always contains 0 (all weights at 0).
@@s020 one vector → a <mark class="dato">line</mark>; two not collinear → a <mark class="dato">plane</mark>; three not coplanar → the <mark class="dato">3D space</mark>
@@s021 Each "new" vector adds a direction. One that is already in the span adds nothing.
@@s022 Ax = x₁a₁ + x₂a₂ + … ⇒ the reachable b = the <mark class="ok">column space</mark> of A
@@s023 Ax = b has a solution <b>only if b is in the span of the columns</b>. Example from the book: A = [[1, 2], [1, 0], [3, 2]] has two columns in 3D: its column space is a plane inside the space.
@@s024 Vector space and subspace, in one line
@@s025 A <b>vector space</b> is a set where sums and multiples of vectors remain in the set (the 8 rules of the book, not to be learned by heart). A <b>subspace</b> is a piece that respects the same rule: for example a line or a plane <b>that pass through the origin</b>. A line that does not pass through 0 is not a subspace.
@@s026 A line, then the plane, then nothing new
@@s027 Linear independence
@@s028 Definition
@@s029 v₁, …, vₙ are <b>linearly independent</b> if the only way to obtain 0 is with all weights at 0: <span class="m">c₁v₁ + … + cₙvₙ = 0 ⇒ c₁ = … = cₙ = 0</span>. Put in words: none can be written as a combination of the others.
@@s030 Example 10.1 of the book: u = (2, 4), v = (1, 2)
@@s031 u is a multiple of v: same line.
@@s032 I found non-zero weights (1 and −2) that give 0: they are <b>dependent</b>.
@@s033 With u and v as columns: determinant 0, a system Ax = 0 with non-zero solutions. Verified with NumPy.
@@s034 Columns of A (square)
@@s035 det A
@@s036 Ax = b
@@s037 <b>independent</b>
@@s038 one single solution, for every b; A⁻¹ exists
@@s039 <b>dependent</b>
@@s040 none or infinitely many solutions; A⁻¹ does not exist
@@s041 More vectors than dimensions ⇒ dependent
@@s042 In ℝⁿ there are at most n independent vectors. A matrix with more columns than rows necessarily has dependent columns. In models: more parameters than training points ⇒ infinitely many solutions (the polynomial of degree 4 with 3 points, <a href="w04-polinomi.html">polynomials</a>).
@@s043 1·u − 2·v = 0: dependent
@@s044 Basis and dimension
@@s045 Basis
@@s046 A <b>basis</b> of a space is a set of vectors that (1) are <b>independent</b> and (2) <b>generate</b> the whole space (span = space). Then every vector is written in <b>only one way</b> as their combination: the weights are its <b>coordinates</b> in that basis. The number of vectors of a basis is the <b>dimension</b>: 2 in ℝ², 3 in ℝ³.
@@s047 The standard basis of ℝ² is e1 = (1, 0), e2 = (0, 1): put as columns they form the identity matrix I. But <b>any</b> pair of independent vectors is a basis of the plane.
@@s048 The same point in two bases
@@s049 Change of basis
@@s050 From basis A to basis B passing through the standard one
@@s051 Coordinates in basis A → standard coordinates: one multiplies by the matrix that has the vectors of A as columns. It is the linear combination (5/3)a1 + (2/3)a2 of the scene.
@@s052 The reverse journey. A⁻¹ always exists, because the vectors of a basis are independent.
@@s053 From A to the standard one (A), then from the standard one to B (B⁻¹). T is the <b>change-of-basis matrix</b>. Backwards: x<sub>A</sub> = T⁻¹x<sub>B</sub> = A⁻¹B x<sub>B</sub>.
@@s054 Bertha: B with columns (1, 1), (−1, 1). T = [[1.5, 1.5], [−0.5, 0.5]] T · (5/3, 2/3) = <mark class="ok">(3.5, −0.5)</mark>
@@s055 Anders tells Bertha the position of Peter without going through (4, 3). Check: 3.5·(1, 1) − 0.5·(−1, 1) = (4, 3). All verified with NumPy.
@@s056 Orthonormal basis: no inverse needed
@@s057 If the vectors of the basis have length 1 and are perpendicular to each other (orthonormal), the matrix Q has <span class="m">QᵀQ = I</span>, so <span class="m">Q⁻¹ = Qᵀ</span>: each coordinate is simply an <a href="w02-prodotto-interno.html">inner product</a> with a vector of the basis. PCA chooses precisely an orthonormal basis suited to the data (week 12).
@@s058 From Anders to Bertha with T = B⁻¹A
@@s059 Why they are needed in machine learning
@@s060 Idea
@@s061 Where
@@s062 <b>independent columns</b> in the design matrix ⇒ XᵀX invertible ⇒ a single least squares solution
@@s063 regression. With a doubled column (x and 2x): rank 2 instead of 3, <code>np.linalg.inv(X.T @ X)</code> → <code>LinAlgError: Singular matrix</code> (verified). Two features that say the same thing add no information.
@@s064 <b>column space</b>
@@s065 least squares projects y onto the span of the columns of X (<a href="w05-minimi-quadrati.html">least squares</a>)
@@s066 <b>bases of functions</b>
@@s067 [1, x, x²] is a basis of the polynomials of degree 2: the model is a linear combination of basis functions (<a href="w05-kernel.html">kernel</a>)
@@s068 <b>change of basis</b>
@@s069 describe the data with more useful axes; with few basis vectors one reduces the dimensions: PCA (Q10)
@@s070 <b>transformations</b>
@@s071 a matrix sends the standard basis to its columns (<a href="w04-trasformazioni.html">transformations</a>)
@@s072 Q4 (d)
@@s073 "Describe vector space, basis, independence and how these are related to concepts in machine learning (learning, transformations etc)." Path: span and linear combinations → independence → basis and coordinates → change of basis (T = B⁻¹A) → design matrix with independent columns and unique solution → towards PCA.
@@s074 A dependent column makes XᵀX singular
